  71	This will effectively make all dev tools available in your current shell session as well as non-interactive environments.
  72	
  73	## Lazy tools
  74	
  75	Set `lazy = true` on a tool when it should be installed the first time one of
  76	its commands is invoked instead of by a bare `mise install`:
  77	
  78	```toml
  79	[tools]
  80	node = { version = "24", lazy = true }
  81	```
  82	
  83	For registry shorthands, mise creates bootstrap shims from the registry's
  84	`bins` metadata. Explicit backends and tools that are not in the registry must
  85	declare their command names with `lazy_bins`:
  86	
  87	```toml
  88	[tools]
  89	"github:example/acme" = { version = "1.2.3", lazy = true, lazy_bins = ["acme", "acmectl"] }
  90	```
  91	
  92	Run `mise reshim` after editing a lazy declaration directly. Commands such as
  93	`mise use` that update tool configuration rebuild the farm automatically.
  94	Invoking a lazy shim installs only its configured provider and then executes
  95	it. This is independent of `not_found_auto_install`; an explicit project tool
  96	selection is never bypassed by a lower-precedence lazy declaration.
  97	
  98	A bare `mise install` skips missing lazy tools. Naming one explicitly, such as
  99	`mise install node`, installs it immediately. Once installed, normal
 100	`mise activate` places the real tool path ahead of the shim farms, so later
 101	calls have no shim dispatch overhead. `mise activate --shims` remains
 102	project-aware and dispatches every call through mise by design.
 103	
 104	::: tip
 105	[`mise activate --shims`](/cli/activate.html#shims) is a shorthand for adding the shims directory to PATH.
 106	:::
 107	
 108	## How to add mise shims to PATH
 109	
 110	The recommended way to add `shims` to `PATH` is to call [`mise activate --shims`](/cli/activate.html#shims) in one of your shell initialization file. For example, you can do the following:
 111	
 112	::: code-group
 113	
 114	```sh [bash]
 115	# note that bash will read from ~/.profile or ~/.bash_profile if the latter exists
 116	# ergo, you may want to check to see which is defined on your system and only append to the existing file
 117	echo 'eval "$(mise activate bash --shims)"' >> ~/.bash_profile # this sets up non-interactive sessions
 118	echo 'eval "$(mise activate bash)"' >> ~/.bashrc       # this sets up interactive sessions
 119	```
 120	
 121	```sh [zsh]
 122	echo 'eval "$(mise activate zsh --shims)"' >> ~/.zprofile # this sets up non-interactive sessions
 123	echo 'eval "$(mise activate zsh)"' >> ~/.zshrc    # this sets up interactive sessions
 124	```
 125	
 126	```sh [fish]
 127	echo 'mise activate fish --shims | source' >> ~/.config/fish/config.fish
 128	echo 'mise activate fish | source' >> ~/.config/fish/config.fish
 129	```
 130	
 131	:::
 132	
 133	In this example, we use [`mise activate --shims`](/cli/activate.html#shims) in the non-interactive shell configuration file (like `.bash_profile` or `.zprofile`) and `mise activate` in the interactive shell configuration file (like `.bashrc` or `.zshrc`)
 134	
 135	::: info
 136	It's fine to call [`mise activate --shims`](/cli/activate.html#shims) in your shell profile file and then
 137	later call [`mise activate`](/cli/activate.html) in an interactive session. PATH
 138	activation keeps the user and existing system shim farms behind real tool
 139	paths. This makes lazy bootstrap commands available without adding dispatch
 140	overhead after installation. `not_found_auto_install` still controls general
 141	missing-tool installation, but does not disable an explicit `lazy = true`
 142	declaration.
 143	
 144	:::
 145	
 146	::: info
 147	When a shim cannot resolve a mise-managed tool (for example, a version pinned in `mise.toml` that hasn't
 148	been installed and [`not_found_auto_install`](/configuration/settings.html#not_found_auto_install) is
 149	disabled), it falls back to the first same-named executable found elsewhere on `PATH` rather than erroring.
 150	This is convenient for tools you also want available outside of mise, but for a tool the OS also ships
 151	(`python3` on Debian/Ubuntu, for example) it means the shim can silently run a completely different,
 152	unrelated binary instead of failing loudly.
 153	
 154	Set [`not_found_system_fallback`](/configuration/settings.html#not_found_system_fallback) to `false`,
 155	alongside `not_found_auto_install = false`, if you'd rather an unresolvable shim fail outright.
 156	:::
 157	
 158	- You can also decide to use only `shims` if you prefer, though this comes with some [limitations](/dev-tools/shims.html#shims-vs-path).
 159	- An alternative to [`mise activate --shims`](/cli/activate.html#shims) is to use `export PATH="$HOME/.local/share/mise/shims:$PATH"`. This can be helpful if `mise` is not yet available at that point in time.
 160	
 161	### mise reshim
 162	
 163	To force `mise` to update the content of the `shims` directory, you can manually call `mise reshim`.
 164	
 165	Use `mise reshim --system` for the system shim farm. If `shims_dir` and
 166	`system_shims_dir` resolve to the same physical path, either command reconciles
 167	one combined farm containing both scopes.
 168	
 169	Note that `mise` already runs a reshim anytime a tool is installed/updated/removed, so you don't need to use it for those scenarios. It is also done by default when using most tools such as `npm`.
 170	
 171	`mise reshim` only creates/removes the shims. Some users sometimes use it as a
 172	"fix it" button, but it is only necessary if `~/.local/share/mise/shims` doesn't contain something it should.
 173	
 174	Do not add additional executable in the `mise` directory, `mise` will delete them with the next reshim.
 175	
  95	- Setting: `shims_dir`
  96	- Environment override: `MISE_SHIMS_DIR`
  97	
  98	The setting is global-only, expands `~`, and must resolve to an absolute path.
  99	
 100	## System installs and shims
 101	
 102	System installs default to `/usr/local/share/mise/installs` and system shims
 103	default to `/usr/local/share/mise/shims`. Their locations can be changed with
 104	the global-only `system_installs_dir` and `system_shims_dir` settings or the
 105	`MISE_SYSTEM_INSTALLS_DIR` and `MISE_SYSTEM_SHIMS_DIR` environment variables.
 106	Both paths expand `~` and must resolve to absolute paths.
 107	
 108	`mise install --system` writes to the system install root and
 109	`mise reshim --system` rebuilds the system farm. Mise never elevates privileges
 110	automatically. Preinstall with the necessary privileges or redirect these
 111	settings when the defaults are not writable.
 112	
 113	Distributions may collocate system and user storage while retaining system
 114	configuration. For example, Omarchy can keep every tool artifact in the user's
 115	home directory:
 116	
 117	```toml
 118	[settings]
 119	system_installs_dir = "~/.local/share/mise/installs"
 120	shims_dir = "~/.local/share/mise/shims"
 121	system_shims_dir = "~/.local/share/mise/shims"
 122	```
 123	
 124	When the shim paths are equal, mise manages one union farm with one lock. When
 125	the install roots are equal, the root is treated as local storage rather than
 126	being scanned and classified twice.
 127	
 128	### `~/.local/share/mise/command-wrappers/bin`
 129	
 130	This is where mise places dispatch shims configured by `[wrappers]`. Mise manages
 131	this directory; use `mise reshim` after adding or removing a command wrapper.

